<Project>
  <!--
    The Unity reference assemblies both local type-check gates compile against, in ONE place.

    #553: `npm run typecheck:unity` answered CS0592 for `[WShowIf]` on a property, and was WRONG.
    Its `UnityEngine.PropertyAttribute` is `AttributeTargets.Field`; every editor CI runs says
    `Property, Field`, and six package attributes inherited that. A confident red for a Unity nobody
    ships is the worse failure mode, because nothing in the output said which Unity answered.

    The pin cannot be moved. `UnityEngine.Modules` has published exactly ONE version, 2021.3.33
    (nuget.org flat-container index, checked 2026-08-24), and the only other community feed of Unity
    reference assemblies, `unity3d.unityengine`, stops at 2020.3.21, older still. So "move the pin
    up to the oldest editor CI runs" and "add a second configuration at the newest editor" are
    impossible rather than merely expensive.

    What is left is to SAY what answered, where the answer is read, which is why the target below
    runs on every compile. TypeCheck and TestCheck used to carry the version separately; they now
    share it, so the two cannot drift and the banner cannot outlive the pin.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UnityReferenceAssemblyVersion>2021.3.33</UnityReferenceAssemblyVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="UnityEngine.Modules" Version="$(UnityReferenceAssemblyVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Target Name="AnnounceReferenceAssemblyPin" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <Message
      Importance="High"
      Text="[typecheck] Reference assemblies: UnityEngine.Modules $(UnityReferenceAssemblyVersion) -- older than every editor CI runs. Member signatures are safe to check here; anything resolved out of Unity's own METADATA (attribute targets, defaults, serialization behaviour) must be confirmed in a real editor. See issue #553."
    />
  </Target>
</Project>
